{{also|abnégation}}
==English==

===Etymology===
First attested before 1398. From {{inh|en|enm|abnegacioun}}, borrowed from {{der|en|LL.|abnegātiō}}, from {{m|la|abnegō||refuse, deny}}, from {{m|la|ab||off}} + {{m|la|negō||deny; refuse, say no}}. Compare {{cog|fr|abnégation}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌæbnɪˈɡeɪʃən/|/ˌæbnəˈɡeɪʃən/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-abnegation.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃən|s=4}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[denial]]; a [[renunciation]]; denial of desire or self-interest. {{defdate|First attested around 1350 to 1470.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=6}}</ref>
#: {{syn|en|repudiation|self-denial|denial|renunciation}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=With '''abnegation''' of God, of his honor, and of religion, they may retain the friendship of the court.|author=w:John Knox|title=Letter to the Queen Dowager|year=1558}}
#* {{RQ:Bindloss Dust of Conflict|chapter=20|passage=Tony's face expressed relief, and Nettie sat silent for a moment until the vicar said “It was a generous impulse, but it may have been a momentary one, while in the case of monk and crusader there must have been a sustaining purpose, and possibly a great '''abnegation''', a leaving of lands and possessions.”}}

